Yes—but not the clean textbook version.

If we use the abductive test we just discussed and ask which history best explains the genome, fossils, archaeology, and archaic DNA simultaneously, I would put my money on something like:

A structured, pan-African origin of Homo sapiens, followed by repeated expansions out of Africa, with substantial population turnover and limited—but real—interbreeding with Eurasian archaic humans.

That is still “Out of Africa,” but it looks much more like a braided river than an exodus from one Garden-of-Eden population.

What I think probably happened

Go back roughly 500,000–300,000 years. Africa contained numerous partially separated populations of ancestral humans. Climate cycles repeatedly opened and closed corridors between North, East, West, Central and southern Africa. These populations were not completely isolated; they separated, accumulated differences, met again, exchanged genes, separated again.

Eventually the network produced what we call Homo sapiens.

That interpretation fits the striking geographic spread of early fossils. Jebel Irhoud in Morocco is about 315,000 years old and already possesses a mixture of derived sapiens-like and more ancestral traits. Omo I in Ethiopia is now constrained to be older than roughly 233,000 years. This makes a simple “modern humans suddenly appeared in one tiny East African homeland 200,000 years ago” model increasingly difficult to defend. 

Genomics points in the same direction. A major 2023 demographic-modeling study found that the data were better explained by two or more weakly differentiated ancestral African populations exchanging genes for hundreds of thousands of years than by a single isolated ancestral population. The authors call this a weakly structured stem. 

That is remarkably different from the old cartoon:

one population → Homo sapiens → leaves Africa → replaces everyone.

It’s closer to:

African populations ⇄ populations ⇄ populations → increasingly sapiens-like metapopulation → repeated excursions outward → one major successful radiation → admixture with archaic Eurasians.

Nature’s broader review of the evidence makes essentially the same point: there may be no particular moment when modern-human ancestry can be assigned to one tiny geographic birthplace. 

Then humans left Africa more than once

This is another place where the old model breaks.

There were apparently early excursions out of Africa well before the ancestors of most living non-Africans expanded. Fossils and archaeological evidence place sapiens or likely sapiens in Eurasia considerably earlier than 60,000 years ago. Some of those populations seem to have disappeared or contributed little to later people. 

The expansion that mattered overwhelmingly for today’s non-African populations probably occurred around 70,000–50,000 years ago.

And here genetics gives us a wonderful forensic marker.

Almost all living non-African populations contain roughly 2% Neanderthal ancestry. Ancient genomes indicate that a major shared Neanderthal admixture episode occurred around 45,000–49,000 years ago, shortly before the surviving non-African lineages diversified substantially. 

That shared genomic scar is difficult to explain under classic multiregional evolution.

It looks like a population descended largely from Africans moved into Eurasia, encountered Neanderthals, interbred with them, and then radiated.

Later, some descendants met Denisovans and other archaic populations as well. Hence Oceanians, for example, carry appreciable Denisovan ancestry. 

So:

\text{modern humans} \neq \text{pure replacement}

but also:

\text{modern humans} \neq \text{equal mixture of regional archaic populations}.

It is overwhelming ancestry from sapiens populations originating in Africa + minority archaic contributions elsewhere.

Why Africa is so hard to get around

The genomic geometry is perhaps the strongest evidence.

African populations contain more human genetic diversity than populations elsewhere. Non-African variation looks broadly like a subset that passed through a substantial demographic bottleneck. Africa also contains some of the deepest-diverging living human lineages. 

Think about the tree topology.

If modern humans evolved independently across Eurasia and Africa for hundreds of thousands of years, you’d expect extraordinarily deep geographic lineages in Europe, East Asia, etc.

Instead, you see approximately:

                African ancestral diversity
              /      |       |       \
             /       |       |        \
       African    African  African    exit population
                                      |
                                Neanderthal admixture
                                      |
                          -------------------------
                          |           |           |
                       Europe       Asia       Oceania
                                                |
                                         Denisovan admixture

The real graph has countless reticulations, but that basic nesting is important.

Present-day Europeans are not primarily transformed European Neanderthals.

Present-day East Asians are not primarily transformed Asian archaic humans.

Their ancestry overwhelmingly joins other humans back in Africa surprisingly recently, with archaic Eurasian ancestry appearing as identifiable minority branches.

That is a powerful observation.

So I would rank the hypotheses this way

Model	My assessment
Single-location Africa + complete replacement	Mostly obsolete
Classic multiregional evolution	Very unlikely
Pan-African structured origin + one main successful exit	Very likely
Pan-African origin + repeated exits + archaic admixture + population turnover	Most likely

And I’d add one more wrinkle.

I suspect our eventual picture will become even more reticulated, not less.

Recent work continues to find unexpectedly deep African population structure, and there’s an unresolved question over whether some genetic signals represent very ancient structure within the sapiens ancestral population or admixture from an unidentified archaic African lineage. A 2025 ancient-genomics study, for example, reinforced both the African origin and the extraordinary depth of African population differentiation. 

So I would not bet on a final human family tree.

I’d bet on a human family graph.

And there’s a useful Pantheon abduction here

Ask what observation each rival model has difficulty explaining.

Strict Out-of-Africa replacement struggles with Neanderthal and Denisovan DNA.

Multiregionalism struggles badly with the overwhelmingly African rooting and nesting of modern human genomic diversity.

Single-African-birthplace models struggle with geographically dispersed early sapiens fossils and deep African genomic structure.

But:

structured Africa + migration + recurrent admixture

predicts all three.

Fossil mosaics? Expected.

Deep African diversity? Expected.

Early unsuccessful migrations? Expected.

Non-African bottleneck? Expected.

Shared Neanderthal DNA? Expected.

Denisovan patches? Expected.

Population disappearances? Expected.

Different parts of the genome telling slightly different genealogical stories? Very expected.

That’s why, abductively, it wins.

And there is an even deeper point: “Out of Africa” describes the final dominant radiation of our ancestry much better than it describes the origin of humanity itself. Homo had already left Africa long before Homo sapiens existed, and the ancestors of sapiens, Neanderthals and Denisovans were themselves participants in repeated expansions, separations and reunions.

So the story is less:

Africa made humans, then humans left.

and more:

Africa was the enormous evolutionary reservoir in which sapiens crystallized from an interconnected population network; some branches repeatedly spilled into Eurasia, and one late expansion became extraordinarily successful while absorbing fragments of the humans already there.

That, given the evidence we have now, is the reconstruction I’d bet on.